# Optional. Enables the shared "micro-AI" tier for small helper tasks —
# download filenames, example prompts, and describing a selected source image
# or video into the generation prompt — so they work without a user's own
# Gemini key. Unlike every other credential in this app this one is server-side
# and app-owned: you pay for it, and it is never sent to the browser.
# Source description uses a vision model on this same token. Leave it unset
# and that action falls back to the user's Gemini key. There is no
# deterministic description, so with neither configured the button asks for a
# Gemini key.
#
# Create a token with Inference permissions at:
#   https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens
#
# Leave it unset and helper tasks fall back to the user's Gemini key, then to a
# deterministic regex slugifier. Nothing breaks either way.
HF_TOKEN=

# Optional. Override the OpenAI-compatible endpoint the micro-AI tier calls.
# Defaults to https://router.huggingface.co/v1/chat/completions
# HF_BASE_URL=

# Turns the gate on. Set this to your own address and the routes that spend the
# app's money — /api/example, /api/slug, and the generation routes — require an
# approved account. This address is approved and made admin on sign-up, so there
# is someone who can approve everyone else. Leave unset and the app stays open,
# exactly as before.
AUTH_ADMIN_EMAIL=

# Leave unset on serverless hosts (the live Vercel deployment included): the
# account store below is a local SQLite file, and an ephemeral per-instance
# filesystem loses accounts and sessions between invocations. See
# docs/deployment.md.

# Where the SQLite file lives. Defaults to ./data/auth.db, which is gitignored.
# Requires Node 22.5+ for the built-in node:sqlite module.
# AUTH_DB_PATH=

# Absolute path to the ffmpeg binary (e.g. /usr/bin/ffmpeg). Enables
# server-side timeline rendering at POST /api/timeline/render. Leave unset and
# the route 404s outright and the export panel reports the server engine as
# unavailable — it never becomes a live, unauthenticated ffmpeg endpoint by
# accident. A path rather than a boolean, so the deployment states explicitly
# which binary it is spending CPU on, and no PATH lookup happens inside a
# route. Still gated by AUTH_ADMIN_EMAIL above when that is set.
#
# Only meaningful on a host with a persistent filesystem and one long-lived
# process: the job registry is in-memory and inputs/outputs live in tmpdir, so
# the render's three requests must all reach the same instance. Leave unset on
# serverless — exports then run in the browser on WebCodecs. docs/deployment.md
# sketches what making the route portable would take.
TIMELINE_FFMPEG_PATH=

# Reverse-proxy note, not an app setting: nginx's client_max_body_size
# defaults to 1 MB, which rejects essentially every real clip upload to
# /api/timeline/render with a 413 long before ffmpeg or proxy_read_timeout
# ever get involved. Raise it on the server block that fronts this app, e.g.:
#   client_max_body_size 1024m;
# The app enforces its own upload ceiling regardless (see MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES in
# app/api/timeline/render/route.ts), so this is about not rejecting valid
# uploads before they reach the app, not about trusting the proxy's limit.
